CONVENTIONAL VACUUM vs ZPF-AS-MEDIUM
530.12 Quantum field theory; vacuum structure

TYPES
-----
dimensional constants : hbar, c, e, epsilon_0
dimensionless constant : alpha
local observables     : atomic spectra, clocks, light travel
frameworks            : QFT + GR   vs   SED / Setterfield-style ZPF

VARIABLES / SYMBOLS
-------------------
hbar      reduced Planck constant          [J s]
c         speed of light in vacuum         [m s^-1]
e         elementary charge                [C]
epsilon_0 vacuum permittivity              [F m^-1]
alpha     fine-structure constant          [dimensionless]

alpha = e^2 / (4 pi epsilon_0 hbar c)

ZPE / ZPF   zero-point energy / zero-point field
SED         stochastic electrodynamics
EEP / LPI   Einstein equivalence principle / local position invariance


FUNDAMENTALS
------------
1. Only dimensionless combinations are unambiguously observable.
   Coordinated changes in hbar and c that keep alpha fixed
   can often be absorbed into units.

2. Local position invariance:
   a local inertial observer measures the same local physics
   (same hbar, same c) everywhere, after ordinary GR redshift
   and time dilation are removed.

3. QFT vacuum effects (Casimir, Lamb shift, vacuum polarization)
   are calculated ON TOP OF fixed constants.
   They do not redefine hbar or c from place to place.

4. Existing GC bound (S-star spectroscopy near Sgr A*):
   Delta alpha / alpha  <~  10^-5 to 10^-6
   This constrains the combination that appears in atomic lines,
   not hbar and c separately.


TWO PICTURES
------------

A. CONVENTIONAL  (QFT + GR)
   Vacuum is not empty, but it is operationally transparent.
   Local laws do not depend on location.
   Gravity bends paths and shifts frequencies.
   Gravity does not thicken the vacuum and thereby
   raise hbar and lower c.
   Prediction: locally measured hbar and c at the
   galactic center equal the terrestrial values.

B. ZPF-AS-MEDIUM  (SED / post claim)
   Vacuum is a real stochastic EM background.
   Local ZPE density ("thickness") SETS the constants.
   Higher density  =>  hbar increases, c decreases,
   alpha held fixed by compensating changes in
   hbar, c, and epsilon_0.
   Prediction: measure hbar at Milky Way center;
   it is higher than in the local neighborhood.


WHY THEY DIVERGE
----------------
Shared slogan : "the vacuum is not empty"
Split         : does local vacuum density DEFINE hbar and c?

Conventional  : no  — constants are inputs; vacuum effects sit on them
ZPF-medium    : yes — constants are emergent from local ZPE density

The galactic-center claim is a prediction of picture B.
Picture A does not generate that spatial rescaling.
